 upward, and you'll see that in homework completion, parent attendance, and enrolment in tutoring. - Working-class faith-based communities — Catholic, evangelical, Muslim — where the church or mosque reinforces educational discipline and parents show up because the institution mobilises them. - Military-adjacent families — even lower-rank enlisted families often have a culture of discipline around school and take opportunities like bursaries seriously. - Rural farming communities with strong 4-H or FFA programmes — these often have a deep ethic of showing up and finishing what you start, and they're frequently overlooked for aid. These aren't the poorest of the poor by raw income, but they're often financially strained, and the cultural engine you're looking for is already running. You'd be putting fuel in a vehicle that's already moving, not trying to push-start one that, in your view, won't budge. Would any of those feel like a better fit for your bursary?
