00:17:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's not what I'm saying, and you've actually landed on a really important point — maybe the most important one in this whole discussion. I'm not arguing that no general statement about any group can ever be trusted. I'm arguing something much narrower: when a general statement attributes a *negative internal trait* to an entire group — "they don't care," "they don't value education" — and when there's a strong, well-documented alternative explanation for the same observable behaviour that has nothing to do with that internal trait, then we should be cautious. Especially when the alternative explanation is testable and the negative trait account has historically been used to justify withdrawing resources. That's not a blanket ban on common sense. It's a specific caution about a specific kind of claim in a specific context. You can still make plenty of general statements that hold up: - "This neighbourhood has low parent attendance at school events" — true, observable, no problem. - "Students in this catchment on average submit less homework" — true, observable, no problem. - "Our bursary uptake in this area has been near zero" — true, observable, no problem. Those are factual, behavioural patterns. No disagreement from me. Where we part ways is when you take that last step from behaviour to character: "...and that's because they don't value education." That's not a description of behaviour anymore. It's an inference about what's in their hearts. And that inference is shaky — not because all inferences are shaky, but because this specific one collapses when you actually give people the means to act. So common sense isn't impossible. It just needs to stay on the right side of the line between describing what people *do* and judging what they *are*. The former is common sense. The latter is often prejudice disguised as observation. I know we've been at this a long time. I respect that you've kept engaging. I'll genuinely stop here unless you want to continue. And the practical offer is still open.
